<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Henry V, Count Palatine of the Rhine</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1173–<a href="/years/1227">1227</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Henry the Elder of Brunswick · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-welf">House of Welf</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Henry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a>, the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion">Henry the Lion</a>, became count palatine of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> in 1195 through his marriage to Agnes, heiress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conrad-count-palatine-of-the-rhine">Conrad of Hohenstaufen</a>. A brother of Emperor Otto IV, he took part in the Welf struggle for the German throne. The palatinate was granted to the Wittelsbach Louis I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> in 1214, and Henry spent his last years on his Brunswick lands, dying in 1227.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henry-v-count-palatine-of-the-rhine.jpg" alt="Henry V, Count Palatine of the Rhine" width="320" height="516" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wgt_Stifterb%C3%BCchlein_36r.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Henry was the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion">Henry the Lion</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-of-saxony">Duke of Saxony</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, and Matilda, eldest daughter of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-england">Henry II of England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleanor-of-aquitaine">Eleanor of Aquitaine</a>. After his father was deposed by Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a>, he grew up in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. When the family returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 1189 he defended the Welf residence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Braunschweig</a> against the forces of the emperor's son, King Henry VI. Under the peace of the next year he and his younger brother Lothar were held as hostages, and he had to join Henry VI's campaign of 1191 against the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When the emperor fell ill, Henry deserted, fled to Marseille and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, where he falsely announced Henry VI's death and presented himself as a possible successor. Though banned, he became heir to the Rhenish palatinate through his marriage in 1193 to Agnes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a>, a cousin of the emperor. The two were soon reconciled, and Henry accompanied Henry VI on the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">Crusade</a> of 1197.</p><p>After the emperor's sudden death in 1197 Henry first supported his brother Otto IV, then went over to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-of-swabia">Philip of Swabia</a> in 1203. Dividing the Welf family lands with Otto and William of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winchester">Winchester</a>, he took the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> territories around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stade">Stade</a> and Altencelle. After Philip's assassination in 1208 he sided with Otto again and tried, without success, to resist the claims of the archbishops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Having inherited further lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> from his brother William, Henry ceded the palatinate to his son Henry the Younger and moved north. When the son died young, Henry left the Welf properties to his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-the-child">Otto the Child</a>, who became the first Duke of Brunswick-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneburg">Lüneburg</a> in 1235. Henry died in 1227 and is buried in Brunswick Cathedral. His second wife was Agnes of Landsberg, daughter of the margrave Conrad II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lusatia">Lusatia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Henry V, Count Palatine of the Rhine” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Brunswick, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.27,10.52&amp;year=1208" aria-label="Brunswick on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Henry V, Count Palatine of the Rhine"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5548 5616h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5548 5616h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=52.27,10.52&amp;year=1208">What was here in 1208? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
